From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 21:10:32 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: import a client's dmabuf that display cannot scan
 out

Kitty's window went permanently blank on AMD (Radeon 890M, Mesa 26.1.8) the
first time it was floated. A resize made the client reallocate onto a second
modifier, gbm_bo_import refused it, and nothing ever moved it back:

  gbm_wrapper.cc:465] nullptr returned from gbm_bo_import
  gbm_pixmap_wayland.cc:132] Cannot create bo with format=BGRA_8888

Both modifiers are AMD_FMT_MOD, GFX11, 64K_R_X, DCC=1, and they differ only in
the DCC layout:

  144115188348713732  RETILE=1 PIPE_ALIGN=0 INDEP_64B=1 MAX_COMP_BLK=64B
  144115188349188868  RETILE=0 PIPE_ALIGN=1 INDEP_64B=0 MAX_COMP_BLK=256B

DCC_RETILE is drm_fourcc.h's "include two DCC surfaces, one which is rb & pipe
aligned, and one which is not-aligned" -- the not-aligned one being the copy
display hardware can read. The second modifier has one pipe-aligned DCC surface
and so is render-only, which is why it imports under GBM_BO_USE_RENDERING and
never under GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT.

That the import asks for SCANOUT at all is the bug, and it is ours rather than
the compositor's. GetSupportedGbmFlags() asks what flags a *format* supports,
and gbm has no equivalent question about a modifier; in the system-libgbm
branch it can answer only GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT or nothing, because the
GBM_BO_USE_TEXTURING fallback beside it is minigbm-only and gbm_defines.h
defines that flag to 0 everywhere else. So every import demands scanout.

Nothing in this path wants it. brokered_frame_sink.cc already asks for
SHARED_IMAGE_USAGE_DISPLAY_READ and says in a comment why it is not SCANOUT:
an imported window is sampled by the display compositor and nothing more. The
two layers disagreed and the lower one refused. This makes the import agree
with the usage its caller asked for.

Strictly additive: the retry runs only where the current code returns nullptr.
Nothing on the Wayland path reads GbmBuffer::GetFlags() -- its only two callers
are in ui/ozone/platform/drm, which this configuration does not build.

Not reproduced on crux, which is NVIDIA. The decode and the code path are the
evidence; the hardware check belongs on the reporter's machine.
---
 ui/gfx/linux/gbm_wrapper.cc | 43 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 43 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ui/gfx/linux/gbm_wrapper.cc b/ui/gfx/linux/gbm_wrapper.cc
index 6020a6081e961..2aea5aca7ae22 100644
--- a/ui/gfx/linux/gbm_wrapper.cc
+++ b/ui/gfx/linux/gbm_wrapper.cc
@@ -461,6 +461,49 @@ class Device final : public ui::GbmDevice {
     // kept open for the lifetime of the buffer.
     struct gbm_bo* bo = gbm_bo_import(device_.get(), GBM_BO_IMPORT_FD_MODIFIER,
                                       &fd_data, gbm_flags);
+
+    // Retry without GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT.
+    //
+    // GetSupportedGbmFlags() asks the device which flags a *format* supports,
+    // and gbm has no equivalent question to ask about a modifier -- so a
+    // format that is scanout-capable still fails to import when the buffer's
+    // modifier is not. With system libgbm that is every import, because there
+    // GetSupportedGbmFlags() returns GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT or nothing at all: the
+    // GBM_BO_USE_TEXTURING fallback beside it is minigbm-only, and
+    // gbm_defines.h defines that flag to 0 everywhere else.
+    //
+    // AMD's DCC modifiers are the case that found this, and the two of them a
+    // Mesa client picks between differ by exactly this (AMD_FMT_MOD, GFX11,
+    // 64K_R_X, DCC=1 both):
+    //
+    //   144115188348713732  RETILE=1 PIPE_ALIGN=0 INDEP_64B=1 MAX_COMP_BLK=64B
+    //   144115188349188868  RETILE=0 PIPE_ALIGN=1 INDEP_64B=0 MAX_COMP_BLK=256B
+    //
+    // DCC_RETILE means the buffer carries a second, non-pipe-aligned copy of
+    // the DCC surface, which is the one display hardware can read; without it
+    // the single DCC surface is pipe-aligned and display cannot read it at
+    // all. So the first imports under SCANOUT and the second never does, and a
+    // client reallocating across a resize moves between them on its own --
+    // permanently, since nothing moves it back.
+    //
+    // Sampling is all that is needed of an imported client buffer here: it
+    // becomes a SharedImage in a texture quad. Losing SCANOUT for it costs the
+    // chance of an overlay promotion, which is worth strictly more than the
+    // blank surface the refused import produces. Nothing on the Wayland path
+    // reads GetFlags(); its only two callers are in the DRM platform.
+    if (!bo && (gbm_flags & GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT)) {
+      const int sampled_flags =
+          (gbm_flags & ~GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT) | GBM_BO_USE_RENDERING;
+      bo = gbm_bo_import(device_.get(), GBM_BO_IMPORT_FD_MODIFIER, &fd_data,
+                         sampled_flags);
+      if (bo) {
+        VLOG(1) << "gbm_bo_import: modifier " << handle.modifier << " of "
+                << DrmFormatToString(format)
+                << " is not scanout-capable; imported it for sampling only";
+        gbm_flags = sampled_flags;
+      }
+    }
+
     if (!bo) {
       LOG(ERROR) << "nullptr returned from gbm_bo_import";
       return nullptr;
